## Chapter 2: Integration I: APIs, Middleware, and MCP Server Design — Flashcards

| # | Front (Question) | Back (Answer) |
|---|-----------------|---------------|
| 1 | What are the three core primitives exposed by an MCP server? | Resources (contextual data), Tools (callable functions with side effects), Prompts (reusable interaction templates). Exam tip: match the primitive to the use case — read-only data vs. action vs. template. |
| 2 | What is the purpose of an Anti-Corruption Layer (ACL) in legacy system integration? | Translates between a clean domain model (used by Claude/MCP tools) and a legacy system's incompatible model/format, preventing legacy quirks from 'leaking' into the new design. Exam tip: ACL = translation at the boundary, not just a protocol swap. |
| 3 | Adapter pattern vs. Anti-Corruption Layer — what's the distinction? | Adapter converts one interface/protocol to another (e.g., SOAP to REST). ACL goes further, translating the underlying domain model/semantics, not just the interface shape. Exam tip: ACL is a 'thicker' adapter focused on meaning, not just syntax. |
| 4 | Gateway pattern vs. Adapter pattern in enterprise MCP architecture | Gateway = single entry point centralizing cross-cutting concerns (auth, logging, rate limiting) across many services. Adapter = converts one specific interface to another for one integration. Exam tip: Gateway sits in front of MANY services; Adapter wraps ONE. |
| 5 | What are the two main MCP transport mechanisms? | stdio (local subprocess, standard input/output) and Streamable HTTP (network-based, supports remote multi-client access). Exam tip: stdio = local only; HTTP-based = remote/enterprise deployments. |
| 6 | Which JSON-RPC methods does an MCP client call to discover and invoke tools? | tools/list (discover available tools) and tools/call (invoke a specific tool with arguments). Exam tip: resources/list & resources/read, and prompts/list & prompts/get follow the same discover-then-use pattern. |
| 7 | Which Claude API field tells you the model wants to invoke a tool? | stop_reason = "tool_use". Other stop_reason values: "end_turn", "max_tokens", "stop_sequence". Exam tip: tool_use responses include a tool_use content block with name and input. |
| 8 | What does 'least-privilege tool exposure via MCP' mean in practice? | Expose narrowly-scoped tools (e.g., get_order_status) instead of broad/generic ones (e.g., run_any_sql_query), and restrict each tool's input schema and backend permissions to only what's needed. Exam tip: a generic 'call any operation' tool is always a wrong answer on least-privilege questions. |
| 9 | Resources vs. Tools in MCP — when to use which? | Use Resources for read-only contextual data the model can reference (docs, catalogs, files). Use Tools for actions/functions, especially those with side effects (create, update, delete). Exam tip: side effects = Tool, not Resource. |
| 10 | What three fields define an MCP/Claude tool schema? | name, description, and input_schema (JSON Schema defining parameters, types, required fields, enums). Exam tip: precise descriptions and constrained enums reduce malformed tool calls at scale. |
| 11 | Name three backpressure handling techniques for MCP servers fronting legacy systems. | Request queuing, token-bucket/leaky-bucket rate limiting, and circuit breakers (plus exponential backoff on retries). Exam tip: backpressure protects the fragile downstream legacy system, not Claude itself. |
| 12 | Why should enterprise MCP servers avoid a single 'call_any_api_operation' generic tool? | It collapses least-privilege design into full access, removes schema-level validation per-operation, and makes auditing/monitoring individual actions much harder. Exam tip: generic catch-all tools are a classic wrong-answer pattern in exposure-design questions. |
